  "body": "The [timer initialization steps](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#timer-initialisation-steps) do the CSP check for string handlers inside the timer task, after the cleared-ID and scripting-disabled checks:\n\n> Perform EnsureCSPDoesNotBlockStringCompilation(realm, « », handler, handler, timer, « », handler). If this throws an exception, catch it, report it for global, and abort these steps.\n\nWith `Content-Security-Policy: script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'`, Chrome Canary, Firefox Nightly, and Safari TP all appear to do the check synchronously instead:\n\n```js\nconst id1 = setTimeout(\"1\", 0); // 0\nconst id2 = setTimeout(\"1\", 0); // 0\nclearTimeout(id2);\n// two securitypolicyviolation events\n```\n\nPer spec, both calls return a nonzero ID, and only `id1` reports a violation when its task runs.\n\nTo match implementations, I think the check should move next to the Trusted Types check, which #13010 moves out of the task, and return 0 when blocked.\n",
